WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:27.575 --> 00:00:31.746
- Hi everyone, welcome to
today's Homeroom live stream.

00:00:31.746 --> 00:00:34.750
Today we will do an ask me
anything, which is always fun,

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.380
I enjoy it a lot.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:37.640
Before we dive into the questions,

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.480
although I encourage you to
start thinking of questions

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.880
and putting them in the message boards

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.910
on Facebook, or YouTube, or
wherever you're watching this,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.340
we have team members who will
help surface these to me,

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.340
but literally ask anything,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.610
and I also ask team members,
surface whatever shows up,

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.180
even some of the less
traditional questions.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.540
But before we break into it,

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.850
I will give my standard announcement

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.790
reminding folks that Khan Academy

00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:00.390
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.470
That means that no one owns Khan Academy,

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:03.410
I don't own Khan Academy,

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.600
or you own as much of
Khan Academy as I do,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.420
and we can only exist because
of philanthropic donations

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:09.390
from folks like yourself.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.010
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.500
please think about donating.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.890
I also wanna give special thanks

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.230
to several organizations that
have supported Khan Academy,

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.950
pre-COVID, but especially when they saw

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.620
that we were running at a
deficit going into the crisis,

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.840
and our costs were going
up because of server costs,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.250
and new programs, and
courses we wanted to create.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.340
Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.940
AT&amp;T, Fastly, and Novartis,
who helped us close the gap,

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.980
although we're still running at a deficit,

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.590
and donations of all
sizes make a difference.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.360
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.690
please think about making
a donation to Khan Academy.

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.950
So with that, I'm eager
to take your questions

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:49.650
about literally anything.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:53.657
And yeah, ask me about anything.

00:01:53.657 --> 00:01:56.260
And I don't have questions in yet,

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:58.050
so I'll just start talking
about random stuff.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.690
I was looking at that
little starting screen

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:02.590
for the live stream,

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.230
and it reminded me about how
bad my current haircut is.

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:06.960
Not that my previous haircut was good,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.660
but several people have
asked in the past about this,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.220
this haircut was done with a
clipper, in about 80 seconds.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:14.900
The SpaceX launch was about to happen,

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.260
I didn't wanna miss it,
I was in my back yard,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.730
so I took the clipper from my wife,

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:22.620
who is a more accomplished
barber than I am,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:23.770
that's not what she does by training,

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:25.520
she's a rheumatologist,

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.710
but that's how I got this haircut.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:29.220
I know none of you have asked about that,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.780
but I do feel self-conscious about that.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.137
So let's see, from YouTube, Eliot H says,

00:02:34.137 --> 00:02:37.780
"What do you think about lack
of privacy on the internet?

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:41.000
We saw abuse of power in
George Floyd's murder,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.307
do you worry that data collected
by GGL," I guess, Google,

00:02:44.307 --> 00:02:48.150
"and Facebook will be used
to hurt people someday?"

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.573
Wow, that's a very big question, Eliot.

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:53.343
I don't know.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.683
And, you know, regardless of what

00:02:56.683 --> 00:02:59.322
your point of view is on data privacy,

00:02:59.322 --> 00:03:01.970
kinda the genie might be out
of the bottle regardless.

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.900
We have a world now where
everyone's got cell phones,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.330
everyone can do live streams,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:07.950
everyone can records something and put it

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.660
immediately on the internet.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:14.580
Now, for the most part,
we're seeing examples

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.240
of that being used for good.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.850
We're seeing examples of that
being able to document things

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:21.060
that we've heard about,

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.310
but when you see it, you just realize

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.300
how horrific it could be,
like George Floyd's murder,

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.440
or what happened with the
birdwatcher in Central Park.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:31.700
And those are just two of
the more recent examples,

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:33.760
this is happening almost
on a daily basis now,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.893
and that is creating more
transparency, more accountability,

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.900
and that'll probably
drive better behavior,

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.470
because, in the past, people who wanted

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.707
to get away with something would say,

00:03:46.707 --> 00:03:48.010
"Well, you know, no one's gonna notice,"

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:51.220
but now, it might be
someone with a cell phone

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.420
who's able to document it,

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.450
it might be a security camera, you know,

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:57.220
that's on the light post
that's able to document it,

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.960
and so you're a little
bit more conscientious

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.060
about what might get seen or not seen.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.700
So that element seems good, but then,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:08.160
of course, there's another side to it,

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.627
which is, you know, the argument,

00:04:09.627 --> 00:04:12.070
there's always been this tension

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:14.830
between security and observation,

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:17.830
and then privacy, and liberty,

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:22.300
because you could imagine an
Orwellian government one day

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.793
using that to control
who interacts with whom,

00:04:28.002 --> 00:04:31.093
or track where you move, and all of that.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.863
And that could be a very scary thing.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.380
And, there's also, not even talking about

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:40.670
an Orwellian state,

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:42.340
there's worlds where, you know,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.990
you're a young person,
you're in high school,

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:47.700
you're in college, I've
heard young people sometimes

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.090
don't do the most
high-judgment activities.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.810
I might have been guilty
of some of that myself,

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:57.340
and it's not helpful if you're
doing something kinda silly,

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.723
dancing with your friends in a silly way,

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.900
and it gets documented, and
shows up on social media,

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.070
and then you lose all control of it,

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:07.400
and then you've lost your privacy

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.170
and your ability to kind of just,

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:10.500
even if it's something fairly harmless,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.930
but if an employer sees you
dancing in that really silly way

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.700
that might might not be a good thing.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.430
So, yeah, it's a tough, tough situation.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:23.733
I think, as individual citizens,

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:26.560
all I can do, is all you
young people out there,

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.340
be careful of folks recording you,

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:35.150
be careful of your privacy.

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:37.480
You might wanna post a bunch of stuff,

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:39.284
because it'll kinda make
your friends giggle,

00:05:39.284 --> 00:05:40.570
and this and that,

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:43.950
but it can have very
far-reaching consequences.

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.988
You know, there's definitely
examples of adults

00:05:46.988 --> 00:05:50.730
with careers, big careers,

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:53.860
who've made some stupid
tweets or social media posts.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:55.280
Now, for society it might've been good,

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.620
because we realized what was
really going on in their mind,

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.440
and we realized that we
probably don't want them

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:00.830
to be the people entertaining us,

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:03.730
or the people who have that power,

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.370
but you could imagine, you know,

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.747
maybe for some of these people, they said,

00:06:06.747 --> 00:06:08.940
"Wow, I made that really dumb tweet,

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:10.410
and now it ruined my career."

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:11.310
And I think, you know,

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:12.260
that's something for everyone

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:13.888
to be a little bit thoughtful about,

00:06:13.888 --> 00:06:17.310
because it can get so easy
to just kind of be unfiltered

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.350
in those types of settings.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.730
So anyway, I don't know.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:21.160
It's a hard thing that we're all

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:22.190
gonna have to struggle with together,

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:23.360
but I get a sense that

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.880
the genie's already out of the bottle.

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.520
Everyone's got phones,
everyone's got recording devices,

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.490
they can post it onto social media.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:32.045
I'm even afraid of this
whole world of deep fakes

00:06:32.045 --> 00:06:34.210
that is becoming more and more possible,

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:38.650
where you literally could
construct a video of someone,

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:40.100
even if they weren't in the video.

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.360
Which is cool, because you get to see

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.930
Princess Leia in the new Star Wars,

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.550
but it can be scary in
a lot of other things.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.060
So let's see.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:53.557
From YouTube, Orchid
Blossom, I like that name.

00:06:53.557 --> 00:06:56.370
"Why does school seem more
like memorization testing

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.590
than learning fun and useful things?"

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:00.700
That's a good question, Orchid.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.860
So, I think there's two aspects about it.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.900
There's the aspect that you control,

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.900
which is your mindset, that
you take towards school,

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:09.600
and then there's the thing that, I guess,

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.090
all of us collectively can control,

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:12.760
but it's more of a systems change thing,

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:16.380
which is how does school
itself present information,

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.520
or guide you through that information.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:22.360
So, I'll start on what
you, yourself can focus on.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:25.250
I found that even if
you are in a curriculum

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:27.210
that is very focused on memorization,

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:30.851
or what seems like
random steps of process.

00:07:30.851 --> 00:07:33.230
It seems like, how do
you solve this equation,

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:34.799
and it's not going to the intuition,

00:07:34.799 --> 00:07:37.724
there is always some underlying intuition.

00:07:37.724 --> 00:07:39.750
And what I would encourage you,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.160
and I've said this before
in the live stream,

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.200
is remember, this material,

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.190
even though it might seem like
something delivered to you

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:49.260
in a very dry, or disjointed,
or memorization way,

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.670
it usually is saying something

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.070
very profound about the universe,

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:54.160
even if it's fact knowledge,

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:57.060
that fact knowledge is
usually quite useful.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:08:00.010
And so what I would say is remind yourself

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:03.150
that it might not taste so good right now,

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.140
but it's telling you something
deep about the universe.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.620
Ponder it even if the curriculum you're in

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.610
isn't drawing the connections, ponder it.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.100
Take some time, indulge,
think about how that thing

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.750
connects to other things in the universe.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.550
And you know, that's
definitely the attitude

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:18.650
that I took growing up,

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:19.970
and I did just because, I don't know,

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:22.250
maybe that's just how
I thought about things.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:25.070
And you know, for sure, I
used to sit in some classrooms

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:29.110
that felt a little bit
not the most engaging,

00:08:29.110 --> 00:08:30.240
and then I had other classrooms

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:31.400
that were incredibly engaging,

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.160
and the teacher really
drew the connections,

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:36.370
and inspired us, and made us appreciate

00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.313
how beautiful what was
being learned really is.

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:41.590
But when you have that attitude

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:43.250
that regardless of which
classroom you're in,

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:45.470
you're always gonna ponder
it, draw connections,

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.500
try to work through it yourself,

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.210
then it'll all be interesting,

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:50.430
and it won't feel like memorization.

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:53.860
You will actually retain much,
much more of that material.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.990
Now on the other side, what
can the system do different?

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:57.410
This is where we at Khan Academy,

00:08:57.410 --> 00:08:58.990
we are trying our best,

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:01.210
and obviously, we are far from perfect,

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:02.780
there is a curriculum out there,

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:04.755
there are standards out there,

00:09:04.755 --> 00:09:06.960
and smart people can have a hearty debate

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.270
about what should be in
and what should be out.

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:11.550
But I think what we try
to do at Khan Academy

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.820
is try to present it to you in a way

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.520
that one, you can get as
much practice and feedback

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.280
and confidence in the
material as possible,

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.070
but also have fun doing it.

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:23.810
And it could be fun because
of the game mechanics,

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.710
and what's known as extrinsic motivators

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.130
that have nothing to do with the content,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:28.720
but that's just motivating you to do it,

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.140
but hopefully, it's also
fun in the work itself.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:34.000
Hopefully, when you're doing
the exercises on Khan Academy,

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.200
you're like, "Oh! I'm
kind of enjoying this."

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.290
And many of you all have
watched videos on Khan Academy,

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.035
and you'll see, I usually am having fun

00:09:40.035 --> 00:09:41.410
working through the material,

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:43.180
and hopefully that is infectious to you,

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:44.640
and hopefully, when you see the videos,

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.830
and if it's me, or one of our
other content team creators,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:52.240
you see we're modeling for you
that really is interesting,

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:54.380
this really is intuitive,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.300
this really is telling
you something profound

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:57.160
about the universe,

00:09:57.160 --> 00:09:59.850
or introducing you to
a new way of thinking.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:01.890
So, we're gonna try to do our best.

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.830
I think many educators are also very aware

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:04.663
of what you're alluding to,

00:10:04.663 --> 00:10:07.366
and so I've seen educators, more and more,

00:10:07.366 --> 00:10:10.080
doing their best to really
bring the intuition,

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.940
the interactivity, the human
side, the interesting side,

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:16.530
because the subjects
inherently are interesting.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:18.346
You almost have to work
to make them boring.

00:10:18.346 --> 00:10:22.270
So hopefully that happens over time,

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:23.810
and you see more and more experiences

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:24.740
that are more engaging

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:27.873
and less rote memorization, or boring.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:32.650
Let's see, this is from
YouTube, Fuzzy Space,

00:10:32.650 --> 00:10:34.267
I like that username as well.

00:10:34.267 --> 00:10:38.400
"Hi Sal, I teach math,
and one of the things

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.490
I'm struggling to do is
write digital exercises

00:10:41.490 --> 00:10:44.570
for students that assess as proofs.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:45.770
Have you guys at Khan Academy

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:48.510
come up with an ideas on how to do this?"

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.160
Fuzzy Space, you are
asking a very big question

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:53.960
that we at Khan Academy
have struggled with

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:56.750
for many, many years, and
we've tried to do our best.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:10:58.210
You know, for those watching

00:10:58.210 --> 00:10:59.610
who don't know what a math proof is,

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:00.910
some of you might remember those kinda

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.420
two-column proofs in geometry,

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:03.790
and when you took it, it might have seemed

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:06.200
like some random things
to the previous question.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:10.570
But a proof, at it's essence,
is can you start with

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.750
some basic assumptions about the universe,

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.940
which we call our axioms,
things we just assume,

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:17.650
and that from those axioms,

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:19.160
you know, one of the axioms is, I exist,

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.960
I just assume I exist.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.110
It's kinda hard to prove or disprove.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:24.640
I think, therefore I am, whatever,

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.000
but it's an axiom that I exist.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.230
But from basic axioms, can you build,

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:33.240
can you deduce other
truths about the universe,

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.080
assuming that those axioms are true?

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:38.030
And that's really one of the
powers of real mathematics.

00:11:38.030 --> 00:11:39.780
You know, Abraham Lincoln famously,

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.450
intimately learned all
of Euclid's elements,

00:11:43.450 --> 00:11:45.580
which is what modern geometry is based on,

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:46.680
and even though we're talking about

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:48.190
something that's 2000 years old,

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:49.840
because he realized it made him

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.260
a much more rigorous thinker,

00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:52.580
and made him a better lawyer,

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:54.060
and made him a better debater.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.450
And so that's what proofs is all about,

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:59.700
just for those of you all who
aren't math-y, so to speak.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:01.810
What's hard about proofs is,

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.892
it's inherently a creative process.

00:12:03.892 --> 00:12:05.667
Whenever people say, like,

00:12:05.667 --> 00:12:07.690
"Oh, well you can do math this way,

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:09.870
but creative subjects
you have to do that way,"

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:12.080
and I say, "Well, no, math
is very creative as well."

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:13.170
If you talk to any mathematician,

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:15.347
they're coming up with
new and novel proofs

00:12:15.347 --> 00:12:17.100
that no one has ever thought about,

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:20.300
new ways of deducing some
truth about the universe.

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:22.560
And ideally we could build that skill

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:23.930
in as many students as possible,

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:25.180
even if they don't become mathematicians,

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:26.680
they, like Abraham Lincoln,

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:28.460
it will improve their
critical thinking skills,

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:29.730
and their ability to think rigorously

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:32.020
about what is for sure true,

00:12:32.020 --> 00:12:33.670
what might be true but is not proven,

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:35.170
and what is for sure not true,

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:39.140
or what might not be true,
but we haven't proven it yet.

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:40.570
The hard thing about anything like that

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:42.390
is it's, you know, multiple choice,

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:44.567
or in a free-response type of format,

00:12:44.567 --> 00:12:46.260
we at Khan Academy, which is,

00:12:46.260 --> 00:12:48.760
it's hard to make that auto-gradable,

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:50.230
or provide auto feedback.

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:52.820
We do attempt to do this
in many of the standards

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:54.860
by, you know, we'll have an exercise

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:57.400
where there's a proof with multiple steps,

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:01.010
and the user has to pick
whether the proof is correct,

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:02.030
and if it isn't correct,

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.300
what step was the logical fallacy made in.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:06.630
So I think that's one area, you know,

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:07.570
we have a couple of things

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:09.950
where you can drag and drop rationales,

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:11.180
especially in geometry,

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:14.500
for why a particular
statement could be right,

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:16.540
and then what could be the
next statement after that.

00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:18.360
But to your point, it's
very, very, very hard.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.610
I think, you know, the ideal is,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.440
it would be similar to
what you'd do in writing.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.620
I mean, there are some AIs
that can grade writing now,

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:28.096
in fact, we use it with our SAT work.

00:13:28.096 --> 00:13:30.130
Maybe you can do something
like that with proofs.

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:31.220
I've heard of some projects,

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.310
I'm not intimately aware of any of them.

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:34.400
I also think there could be something

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.020
around peer review that does it,

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.750
where, imagine you're asked to prove

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:42.330
some statement in mathematics,

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.920
and someone who's already proven it,

00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:46.560
and has been validated that has proven it,

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:47.500
maybe it's an older student,

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:49.460
or maybe it's just another
student in the classroom,

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:50.830
can then look at your proof,

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:53.310
and grade and give you feedback

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:55.220
as to whether it is correct or not.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:56.580
That's an interesting mechanism

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:58.340
that could happen inside the classroom.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:13:59.550
I'm also intrigued about ways

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:03.973
that we could do that in
the broader world as well.

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:10.127
So, from Facebook, Guillermo Acosta says,

00:14:10.127 --> 00:14:11.780
"How do we know your child is ready

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.320
to begin on Khan Academy?"

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:14.470
Great question.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:19.470
Sorry, I'm having some
internal burps, I guess.

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:25.000
How do you know if your child
is ready for Khan Academy?

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.540
So I think there's two major
Khan Academy experiences,

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:29.830
one is Khan Academy kids,

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:32.460
and then there is kind of our website,

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:35.240
which also has an app, and
is available on mobile.

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:39.690
So Khan Academy kids is
designed for ages two to seven.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:44.540
It covers all of the Headstart
and Common Core standards

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:47.220
for pre-K, Kindergarten, and first grade

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:50.440
in math, reading, writing,
and social/emotional learning.

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:52.840
And so, if you have someone,

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:55.875
especially between ages two and six,

00:14:55.875 --> 00:14:58.740
who is kind of at their
developmental level,

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:00.490
I highly recommend Khan Academy Kids.

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:03.130
It's highly interactive,
it's tablet or phone based,

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:04.900
so kids don't have to
know how to use a mouse,

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:08.450
or a keyboard, or read
directions and all that.

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:13.000
I would say our core Khan
Academy, or big Khan Academy,

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:14.860
whatever you wanna call about it,

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:19.363
I think, for third grade
and up, it is very usable.

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:21.370
The reason why I say third grade

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:24.973
is because Khan Academy does presuppose,

00:15:25.861 --> 00:15:27.830
if you're using it online, on the web,

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:30.750
it presumes some use of the
mouse, and the keyboard.

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:32.635
And even if you're using
it on a mobile device,

00:15:32.635 --> 00:15:34.120
even if you're using the app,

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:38.060
it does require some, enter the equation,

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.700
or it says stuff like
"Select all that apply,"

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:43.920
and to a kindergartner,
select all that apply,

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:45.530
they might not even be able to read that.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:46.390
Or even a first grader.

00:15:46.390 --> 00:15:50.527
So, I say third grade for sure
is where we've seen a lot of,

00:15:50.527 --> 00:15:51.790
and there's definitely examples

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:53.780
of fairly precocious kindergartners,

00:15:53.780 --> 00:15:55.060
and first graders, and second graders

00:15:55.060 --> 00:15:56.860
who have high reading capability,

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:58.320
who are also able to engage

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.130
on kind of big Khan Academy, so to speak,

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:01.900
starting with our early learning work.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.250
Because our content goes all
the way into early learning.

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:05.870
I would say if you have a student,

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:08.230
a child that's kind of right at the cusp,

00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.280
Khan Academy Kids, the
first grade content's

00:16:10.280 --> 00:16:12.220
feeling a little bit too easy for them,

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.850
so they're ready to
start going into second

00:16:13.850 --> 00:16:17.540
and third grade content on
big Khan Academy, so to speak,

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:19.760
I would recommend for you,

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:21.680
as a parent or loved one or sibling,

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.770
to be able to sit down with that student.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:24.910
And don't give them the answers,

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:25.820
don't tell them how to solve it,

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:28.770
but explain to them when it
says select all that apply.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:30.910
Or if they're having trouble
moving something with a mouse,

00:16:30.910 --> 00:16:32.650
or their motor skills aren't
where they need to be,

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:33.820
that you can help them there.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:35.050
And just also motivate them,

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:37.210
because the game mechanics,
or the motivational mechanics

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:40.190
on the big Khan Academy are not, you know,

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:45.190
100% designed for the six
or seven year old crowd.

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:47.660
So I think having a loved
one next to the child,

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:48.600
working through it,

00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:50.420
I think that's great for
Khan Academy Kids as well,

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.410
but it's especially important
for the younger students

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:56.680
on kind of our non-Khan
Academy Kids Khan Academy.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:59.773
We have to come up with a better
word for big Khan Academy.

00:17:03.429 --> 00:17:05.457
Let's see, Facebook, Kieran Dudlany says,

00:17:05.457 --> 00:17:06.750
"Would love to know Sal's thoughts

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.240
on how to get more girls
interested in STEM subjects,

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:11.210
especially math."

00:17:11.210 --> 00:17:12.420
It's a great question, Kieran,

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:15.020
and I won't claim that
I'm the expert here,

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:18.530
but, you know, I have
anecdotal experience.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:20.250
Many of you know that Khan Academy started

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:23.960
with me tutoring my, at the
time, 12 year old cousin Nadia.

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:27.230
And, in a lot of ways,
what she was going through

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:30.270
was what I think a lot
of girls go through.

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:32.770
She was 12 years old,
she was a good student,

00:17:32.770 --> 00:17:34.210
she was trying to do what's right,

00:17:34.210 --> 00:17:36.570
but she had kind of convinced herself

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:38.250
that she wasn't a math person.

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:40.330
And I don't know where it came from,

00:17:40.330 --> 00:17:42.623
I'm guessing it probably
got from, you know,

00:17:44.063 --> 00:17:46.550
you'll hear your mother say
that you're not into math,

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:51.550
or people gravitate
to, or they see a movie

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:53.240
where they disproportionately see

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:55.640
that the man's the math guy,

00:17:55.640 --> 00:17:58.377
and the woman's the humanities person,

00:17:58.377 --> 00:18:02.304
and so they build these
internal biases on who they are.

00:18:02.304 --> 00:18:06.026
And I do think it's around
11, or 12, or 13 year olds

00:18:06.026 --> 00:18:10.470
that all of us start
really defining who we are,

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:12.330
what are we, quote,
good at, or not good at.

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:14.116
And obviously, that's not
a good attitude to have,

00:18:14.116 --> 00:18:15.180
because that's not a growth mindset.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:17.550
You should say, "I can
be good at anything,

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:19.940
and I can only discover that by failing

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.360
and pushing myself out
of my comfort zone."

00:18:21.360 --> 00:18:25.280
But Nadia was there, and
I remember, back in 2004,

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:28.300
it took a couple of weeks
for me to just deprogram

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:29.690
this self-perception of her

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:31.600
that she just wasn't good at math,

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:32.850
and that was the biggest hurdle,

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:34.940
because once I kinda deprogrammed that,

00:18:34.940 --> 00:18:36.430
she was off to the races.

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:39.140
This was a young girl in seventh grade

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:41.390
who was put into kind
of a slower math track,

00:18:42.603 --> 00:18:45.180
after I deprogrammed that,
or helped deprogram it,

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:47.830
and got her caught up, and
even ahead of her class,

00:18:47.830 --> 00:18:50.210
within a year and a half
she was taking calculus.

00:18:50.210 --> 00:18:52.163
So clearly, she was very capable,

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:55.850
this is as a freshman in high school,

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:57.980
she was very capable at learning math,

00:18:57.980 --> 00:19:00.700
as I think all children, and all girls,

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:02.010
are very capable.

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:04.140
So, I think it's a combination of,

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:06.280
you have to have someone
who truly believes it,

00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:08.250
not just giving them kind of lip service,

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:11.160
truly believes that not
only are they capable,

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:12.950
they should be capable.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:15.410
I also think it takes the time,

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:17.080
and that kind of habit-forming, of,

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:18.930
let's call it 20, 30 minutes day,

00:19:18.930 --> 00:19:20.650
of seeing that if you do that practice,

00:19:20.650 --> 00:19:22.507
and Khan Academy's here for that,

00:19:22.507 --> 00:19:24.200
but if you do that 20, 30 minutes a day,

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.490
no matter where you start,
within a month, two months,

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:28.030
you're going to see real gains.

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:29.330
And likely, you would have caught up

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:31.170
or even surpassed where your class is.

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:32.610
And I should think, you know,

00:19:32.610 --> 00:19:34.030
I've gotten in to pedagogical debates

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:36.610
about whether it's good to
go ahead of class or not,

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:38.760
but I do think for a
student's self-confidence,

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:39.670
if you're a seventh grader

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:40.930
who thought you weren't good at math,

00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:43.100
I saw it with Nadia, if you can get ahead

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:43.987
of where your class is,

00:19:43.987 --> 00:19:45.900
you know, I remember that first time

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:49.870
that I taught Nadia kind
of an algebra topic,

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:51.560
and she picked it up quite fast.

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:53.437
You know, simple solving equations,

00:19:53.437 --> 00:19:55.960
and I said, "You know,
Nadia, what I just did

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:57.980
isn't seventh grade, it's
not even eighth grade,

00:19:57.980 --> 00:19:59.840
it's actually a ninth
grade algebra topic."

00:19:59.840 --> 00:20:01.510
And I remember, still, you know,

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:03.690
kind of her, I could hear in her voice,

00:20:03.690 --> 00:20:05.460
we we're actually doing it over the phone,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.960
but I could kind of hear her
eyes light up over the phone,

00:20:07.960 --> 00:20:10.000
that like, "Really? I, Nadia,

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.340
have just done ninth grade math?

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:14.740
Maybe I'm a math person!"

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:16.190
So I think that could be a fun thing,

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:20.490
is, don't make everything
just about catching up,

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:22.030
or the next test,

00:20:22.030 --> 00:20:25.290
start introducing a few
ideas that are further out.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.204
You know, with my own kids,

00:20:27.204 --> 00:20:29.900
there's ideas from calculus
you could introduce.

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:31.850
The idea of limit, you could introduce,

00:20:31.850 --> 00:20:33.340
actually, to very young kids,

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:35.150
ideas of solving equations, you know.

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:38.223
I talk about this with my
nine year old daughter,

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:40.200
and she gets it.

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:41.820
And when I tell her that actually,

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:43.970
this is kind of a ninth grade concept,

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:48.230
she's like, "Oh, maybe I'm good at this."

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:50.560
And I think that's the kind of
thing that could be helpful.

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:53.880
So, yeah, that's my best advice,

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.460
but I'm definitely not an expert there.

00:20:56.460 --> 00:21:01.460
So, from Facebook, Syme Hur-Hassan says,

00:21:01.487 --> 00:21:03.290
"Hi Sal, what are your views

00:21:03.290 --> 00:21:05.270
on the new policy for
international students?"

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:09.170
So I think wat Syme is
referring to is this policy,

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:10.480
and I only read some of the headlines,

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:12.744
or I read some of the
articles fairly quickly,

00:21:12.744 --> 00:21:15.642
but what I read was this notion that,

00:21:15.642 --> 00:21:18.670
if a student is not attending college,

00:21:18.670 --> 00:21:19.503
an international student

00:21:19.503 --> 00:21:22.580
is not physically attending the campus,

00:21:22.580 --> 00:21:25.403
that they will not get a
student visa to attend.

00:21:27.230 --> 00:21:30.040
I think that's a bad idea,

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:34.376
because it then makes the
schools not think about

00:21:34.376 --> 00:21:37.550
what's right from a
healthcare point of view,

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:40.490
and then, if they say, "Okay,
I guess we're going to have to

00:21:40.490 --> 00:21:43.900
figure out some way for
the kids to be on campus,

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:46.387
because otherwise they're
going to have to leave."

00:21:47.624 --> 00:21:51.430
And so, you know, any time you're creating

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:54.313
a kind of a conflict of
interest, in any space,

00:21:55.577 --> 00:21:57.863
that does not have a clear benefit to it,

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:03.540
I don't get the benefit
of something like this.

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:08.540
So, yeah, that's my view on it.

00:22:11.570 --> 00:22:14.200
And when you issue an edict

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:17.230
that will disrupt many people's lives,

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:19.800
and maybe even put them
in some type of danger

00:22:19.800 --> 00:22:22.223
because there's a pandemic going around,

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:24.660
or even puts professors in danger,

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:26.220
not even just the international students,

00:22:26.220 --> 00:22:27.330
puts the community in danger,

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:28.720
puts the whole country, you know,

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:33.360
because anyone gets coronavirus,

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:35.650
they now become a vector
and spread it to others,

00:22:35.650 --> 00:22:37.783
so that's not a good thing.

00:22:39.929 --> 00:22:41.340
So, that doesn't make sense.

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:43.300
And if you're gonna do
something disruptive like that,

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:44.210
it should have a benefit,

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:46.340
and it's not clear to
me what the benefit is

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:48.170
of sending international students home,

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:51.520
or not allowing them to be here

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:55.610
while taking things distance learning

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.510
if that's what the universities
think is most appropriate.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:00.480
So, yeah, you can imagine,

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:02.780
you're a sophomore at
the local university,

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:04.680
you're an international student,

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:06.060
you're on a student visa,

00:23:06.060 --> 00:23:08.850
and for very good reasons,
epidemiological reasons,

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:10.810
the university has just decided

00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:14.730
that it's best for the fall
to do everything online,

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:16.220
and so you're like, "Yeah, I'll do that,

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:18.030
I'll stay in my rented house

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:20.160
that I'm sharing with other
friends, other students,

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:22.635
and take that course,
and now, all of a sudden,

00:23:22.635 --> 00:23:25.310
I'm hearing that I'm
not allowed to do that,

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:26.850
I will have to leave the country,"

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:28.686
which might have all
sorts of implications.

00:23:28.686 --> 00:23:30.970
Some of it is, it's even
hard to travel right now.

00:23:30.970 --> 00:23:34.900
But anyway, that's my
answer to that question.

00:23:34.900 --> 00:23:37.597
So, from YouTube, Adarsh Prasana says,

00:23:37.597 --> 00:23:40.087
"Is it very hard being a not-for-profit?"

00:23:42.250 --> 00:23:45.300
On some levels, yes, although I think,

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:47.520
to do anything in any way, shape, or form

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:48.790
at scale can be hard,

00:23:48.790 --> 00:23:51.890
and there's always going
to be competing interests.

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:54.710
If you're for-profit,
you have to raise money,

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:57.350
get investors, hire up,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:23:57.350 --> 00:23:59.230
and then, the really hard thing is,

00:23:59.230 --> 00:24:02.190
in some ways, for-profit, it
might be easier to raise money,

00:24:02.190 --> 00:24:03.857
because you can appeal to people's,

00:24:03.857 --> 00:24:07.377
"Hey, I have an idea that
can make you rich," you know,

00:24:07.377 --> 00:24:08.510
"Give me a million dollars

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:09.890
and I'll give you 10% of my company,

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:12.030
and when this becomes
a $10 billion company,

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:13.230
that million will be a billion,"

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:16.690
and people kinda, "Ooh, that's
exciting, here's the money."

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:18.740
The hard part about for-profits, though,

00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:21.270
is because of that motive,

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:23.380
when you wanna do something that you think

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:26.740
is sometimes just the
right moral thing to do,

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:27.930
or it's consistent with your values,

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:29.720
but if it's not consistent,

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:31.160
what maybe some of those investors

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:33.350
think should be your priority,

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:35.690
or what they think is going
to maximize their profit,

00:24:35.690 --> 00:24:37.360
that can sometimes, and you know,

00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:39.117
most investors, I think,
are very good people,

00:24:39.117 --> 00:24:41.560
and they wanna do the right
thing, et cetera, et cetera,

00:24:41.560 --> 00:24:44.640
but the mindset is, look, we're
here to optimize our profit,

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:47.930
we're not here to be, like,
do-gooders, so to speak.

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:49.720
Now, ideally they could do both,

00:24:49.720 --> 00:24:52.040
but that's where it can lead to tensions.

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:55.780
Not-for-profits kinda have the
symmetric opposite problem,

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:57.330
where, everyone knows that like,

00:24:57.330 --> 00:24:58.470
the not-for-profit's very clear,

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:00.420
there's no owners here,
this is our mission,

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:02.860
we're here to do good in
whatever dimension do good is,

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:04.610
for Khan Academy, it's
free world-class education

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:06.580
for anyone, anywhere, you know,

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:09.119
give access to materials,
especially to students

00:25:09.119 --> 00:25:11.770
who otherwise would not have them,

00:25:11.770 --> 00:25:14.170
try to move the dial in
efficacy in learning,

00:25:14.170 --> 00:25:15.880
support teachers, things like that.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:18.960
So the good thing is, when
people donate to Khan Academy,

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:21.423
they know that's what they're donating to.

00:25:21.423 --> 00:25:24.330
We have a board of directors
that holds us accountable,

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:27.140
it's made up of many of our larger donors,

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:29.555
but also just experts in
education, and educators.

00:25:29.555 --> 00:25:32.170
And what I love about our board meetings

00:25:32.170 --> 00:25:36.870
is that they don't say, I
mean, we don't have a profit,

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:37.847
in that nation, but they're saying,

00:25:37.847 --> 00:25:42.600
"Sal, we need to make sure
that these philanthropic funds

00:25:42.600 --> 00:25:44.570
are reaching the kids that need it most,

00:25:44.570 --> 00:25:46.500
we need to be able to measure efficacy,

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:48.960
how do we engage kids who
otherwise would not be engaged,"

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:50.420
that's the conversation, which I love,

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:53.010
because it's super-aligned
with the mission.

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:54.770
The hard part is, is that, you know,

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:57.310
you gotta raise money continuously.

00:25:57.310 --> 00:25:59.560
There are revenue streams we've developed

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:00.810
that are consistent with our goals,

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:02.880
folks like the College Board pay us

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.850
to do a free SAT practice, so that helps.

00:26:05.850 --> 00:26:06.910
We have corporate sponsors,

00:26:06.910 --> 00:26:08.010
but for the most part, you know,

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:09.150
as you saw at the beginning of this,

00:26:09.150 --> 00:26:11.490
I ask a lot of people
for money all the time,

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:13.910
and you know, you gotta kinda
check your ego at the door

00:26:13.910 --> 00:26:16.453
when you're asking for money from people.

00:26:17.870 --> 00:26:19.380
But you know, there's
positives to that too.

00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:22.620
I think when people donate it
brings them into the mission,

00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:25.510
they become ambassadors for
the work that you're doing,

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:28.610
and you know, one of the
fears of not-for-profit is,

00:26:28.610 --> 00:26:31.900
we also, obviously,
don't give people stock,

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:34.943
the way, if you work at
a Google or Facebook,

00:26:35.990 --> 00:26:37.810
you get a nice salary, but then actually,

00:26:37.810 --> 00:26:39.720
most of your compensation
comes through stock,

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.288
especially if the stock is appreciating.

00:26:42.288 --> 00:26:44.250
Well, whatever your salary
is, you might get two, three,

00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:47.230
some times five, 10x
of that through stock.

00:26:47.230 --> 00:26:50.920
And we live, you know,
Khan Academy is competing

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:53.190
in certain areas of the same talent pool.

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:56.210
We have educators, and content
creators, and researchers,

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.370
but we also hire a lot
of engineers, designers,

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:00.473
and product managers,
and we directly compete

00:27:00.473 --> 00:27:02.730
with the Googles and
Facebooks of the world.

00:27:02.730 --> 00:27:04.330
And there was a fear
that we would not be able

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:06.380
to attract the same talent
because we can't give stock,

00:27:06.380 --> 00:27:09.490
because there is no such thing
as stock in Khan Academy.

00:27:09.490 --> 00:27:11.890
The good thing is, we have
been able to attract talent,

00:27:11.890 --> 00:27:14.780
which just shows you
that really great people,

00:27:14.780 --> 00:27:17.080
if you pay them enough
so that they can live

00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:18.890
and send their kids to
school, and college,

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:20.420
and go on vacations,
and eat at a restaurant

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:21.410
every now and then,

00:27:21.410 --> 00:27:23.550
that they actually care
more about mission,

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:24.790
intellectually challenging work,

00:27:24.790 --> 00:27:26.560
and being able to work with
other really impressive

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:28.460
mission-aligned people,
so we're seeing that.

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:32.810
So anyway, I believe anyone
running an organization

00:27:32.810 --> 00:27:34.510
that's a not-for-profit or for-profit

00:27:34.510 --> 00:27:35.660
at the scale of the Khan Academy

00:27:35.660 --> 00:27:37.630
with the reach of a Khan Academy

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:40.590
wouldn't feel relaxed all the time,

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:43.480
but to you're point,
there's definitely struggles

00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:44.390
of a not-for-profit.

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:45.580
You know, my dream is that Khan Academy

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:47.220
is an institution that lasts forever,

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:49.235
that maybe one day there's an endowment

00:27:49.235 --> 00:27:51.650
so that it can last for hundreds of years,

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:53.181
and billions of people,

00:27:53.181 --> 00:27:54.709
because I think the social
return on investment

00:27:54.709 --> 00:27:58.963
is definitely there, so, fingers crossed.

00:28:00.790 --> 00:28:03.530
So, from Facebook, and time goes by fast,

00:28:03.530 --> 00:28:04.370
every time I do this,

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:06.480
I didn't realize we only
have a few minutes left.

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:07.520
Well, I can go a little bit longer,

00:28:07.520 --> 00:28:09.340
there's a lot of really
good questions here.

00:28:09.340 --> 00:28:11.590
From Facebook, Robert
Haggen, or Hagen, says,

00:28:12.613 --> 00:28:14.470
"Do you recommend daily
lessons during summer break?

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:16.450
While I want my son to
enjoy his summer break,

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:18.130
I also know options for summer fun

00:28:18.130 --> 00:28:20.260
are limited during social distancing."

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:25.260
So, my view, and I'm a
self-described tiger-cousin

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:27.700
for my family, that's
how Khan Academy started,

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:31.020
and I have three kids
now, I would say yes.

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:34.650
And, this doesn't mean ruining
your son's summer vacation,

00:28:34.650 --> 00:28:37.840
it means 30 minutes a day of math,

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:40.160
maybe 30 minutes a day of reading,

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:42.270
and maybe 30 minutes a day of writing,

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:44.060
journaling, whatever that is.

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:46.390
And I think if you did
that, and, talk to your son,

00:28:46.390 --> 00:28:49.100
there's nothing, I just
described an hour and a half

00:28:49.100 --> 00:28:52.180
of a very long day where
the sun sets very late,

00:28:52.180 --> 00:28:55.430
and there's plenty of time
for doing other things,

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:56.263
and playing outside,

00:28:56.263 --> 00:28:59.530
and all the things that we
know and love about summer.

00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:01.930
But if he's able to do that,

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:04.130
not only will his skills not atrophy,

00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:06.110
but he's likely to go
into next school year,

00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:07.460
and it goes back to that
confidence question,

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:08.490
whether you're a boy or a girl,

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:12.800
just being more confident,
have a bigger margin of safety,

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:14.880
probably perceive him or her self

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:19.680
more as an academic person who's
capable of learning things.

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.520
So I would absolutely go for it.

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:22.560
And once again, you don't
have to go overboard,

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:23.930
it doesn't have to be
hours and hours a day.

00:29:23.930 --> 00:29:27.030
It can literally be, you know, worst case,

00:29:27.030 --> 00:29:29.670
do half an hour of math a
day, half an hour of reading.

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:30.700
Like, that's not a lot.

00:29:30.700 --> 00:29:32.700
So, if your son is watching,

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:35.170
I think if your dad asks you to do that,

00:29:35.170 --> 00:29:37.470
that is very, very
reasonable of him (laughs).

00:29:41.290 --> 00:29:44.490
So, let's see, we're almost out of time,

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:45.510
so I just want to...

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:47.850
"Does Khan Academy use
adaptive learning technology

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:49.780
that's from Imani Malaika?

00:29:49.780 --> 00:29:51.420
To some degree, we've used it,

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:53.080
and we're trying to do more adaptive.

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:56.820
I could talk to for a
long time about that,

00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:57.910
but I'll go to the next question.

00:29:57.910 --> 00:30:00.107
From YouTube, Vramya Sivikanth,

00:30:00.107 --> 00:30:01.990
"Do you have more than
the Khan Academy website?"

00:30:01.990 --> 00:30:03.863
Well, there's the website,
there's the mobile app,

00:30:03.863 --> 00:30:06.540
and also, the website,
there's also mobile web,

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:08.460
we have Khan Academy kids.

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:10.070
Separate from that, I've mentioned

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:11.370
a couple of times on this forum,

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:13.300
there's a bit of a side
project that I have

00:30:13.300 --> 00:30:14.920
called schoolhouse.world,

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:17.580
which is a very early
stage, volunteer-driven,

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:19.940
non-Khan Academy project, but related,

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:24.510
where it's pairing up students
who need help, live help.

00:30:24.510 --> 00:30:26.620
Let's say you're on Khan
Academy, you're learning,

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:28.410
you're getting practice,
feedback, you're watching videos,

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:30.170
but you still want live help,

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:31.910
you can go to schoolhouse.world,

00:30:31.910 --> 00:30:33.770
right now it's focused on
high school mathematics,

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:35.220
and then there's volunteer tutors

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:36.530
who are running group tutoring sessions

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:37.760
to give folks that live help.

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:39.380
So that's another interesting thing

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:41.390
that's at a very, very early stage.

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:43.117
Let's see, from Facebook, Jackie Tate,

00:30:43.117 --> 00:30:45.240
"What is the best math skills to get ready

00:30:45.240 --> 00:30:47.940
for computer science
section of Khan Academy?"

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:49.340
Well, it's interesting,
the computer science,

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:51.010
the programming section of Khan Academy,

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:52.820
you really don't need
to know a lot of math.

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:54.450
I would say, if you get to kind of

00:30:54.450 --> 00:30:58.070
a fifth or sixth grade
level of arithmetic,

00:30:58.070 --> 00:30:59.980
yeah, if you know your negative numbers,

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:02.530
you're pretty well-versed
with decimals, fractions,

00:31:03.680 --> 00:31:06.430
exponents, I think you're pretty close

00:31:06.430 --> 00:31:08.580
to being able to engage
on our programming.

00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:10.700
It was literally designed
for elementary school,

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:13.810
kind of late elementary
school students, in that mind.

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:15.200
If you have kind of am
middle school level,

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:16.590
or especially even early algebra,

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:18.850
where you're starting to
understand what a variable is,

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:21.330
then I think you're in
very, very, very good shape.

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:22.980
But a lot of ways, you
doing the programming

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:25.166
is a great way to learn what variable is

00:31:25.166 --> 00:31:27.223
for a lot of students.

00:31:29.912 --> 00:31:32.467
VX Lee is asking a very
flattering question,

00:31:32.467 --> 00:31:33.917
"How are you so smart?"

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:37.820
My ego is flattered by that,

00:31:37.820 --> 00:31:40.020
but I will tell you that
there's many times in life

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:43.370
that I have and continue
to feel not-so-smart.

00:31:43.370 --> 00:31:46.270
So, everyone feels impostor
syndrome sometimes,

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:48.960
everyone feels out of
their element sometimes.

00:31:48.960 --> 00:31:51.010
But I've been able to probably build

00:31:51.010 --> 00:31:53.630
more confidence than most in,

00:31:53.630 --> 00:31:55.460
let's call it academic
or intellectual pursuits,

00:31:55.460 --> 00:31:58.110
because, to the very first question,

00:31:58.110 --> 00:31:59.710
I've always been someone to ponder,

00:31:59.710 --> 00:32:01.100
and try to draw connections,

00:32:01.100 --> 00:32:03.760
and when something just
doesn't make sense,

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:05.070
I haven't been afraid to ask

00:32:05.070 --> 00:32:07.520
what some people would
call dumb questions,

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:09.130
and I found that oftentimes,

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:10.390
what you think are dumb questions,

00:32:10.390 --> 00:32:12.810
when you really dig in, and
double click and ask someone,

00:32:12.810 --> 00:32:14.906
they oftentimes can be profound questions,

00:32:14.906 --> 00:32:18.660
and that can make you
build your confidence.

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:21.820
So I would say, it's all about
creating a habit of learning,

00:32:21.820 --> 00:32:24.190
a habit of being curious.

00:32:24.190 --> 00:32:26.836
Keep asking questions, don't be afraid

00:32:26.836 --> 00:32:31.090
to show your vulnerability,
and fill in those gaps,

00:32:31.090 --> 00:32:32.100
because before you know it,

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:34.810
you're gonna be a
polymath that knows a lot

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:36.173
about a lot of subjects.

00:32:37.610 --> 00:32:41.330
And, you know, let's see,
Mohammed Sami Mussaid from YouTube

00:32:41.330 --> 00:32:42.610
asks a similar, "How can you get yourself

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:44.450
to start liking school?"

00:32:44.450 --> 00:32:46.400
Look, in most of human history,

00:32:46.400 --> 00:32:48.430
we have just never had the luxury

00:32:48.430 --> 00:32:50.960
of investing in
understanding the universe.

00:32:50.960 --> 00:32:53.610
Most of human history, we
were running away from things

00:32:53.610 --> 00:32:56.350
or chasing down things for a meal,

00:32:56.350 --> 00:32:58.830
or worried about wars with
the neighboring tribe,

00:32:58.830 --> 00:32:59.690
or something like that,

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:02.340
and so it's actually an incredible luxury

00:33:02.340 --> 00:33:05.840
for us to have something,
and I think it's a necessity,

00:33:05.840 --> 00:33:07.640
but compared to most of human history,

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:09.580
it would have seemed like a luxury

00:33:09.580 --> 00:33:11.460
to be able to have a time of your life

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:13.010
where you could invest, and learn,

00:33:13.010 --> 00:33:14.320
and develop your critical thinking.

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:16.480
And I know it's not
always the most engaging,

00:33:16.480 --> 00:33:18.930
and sometimes it can feel
a little bit demoralizing,

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.990
but when you realize that
you're getting to learn

00:33:21.990 --> 00:33:23.590
human knowledge that had been built up

00:33:23.590 --> 00:33:27.360
over thousands of years,
that Newton, Marie Curie,

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:30.760
the kings of antiquity would have killed,

00:33:30.760 --> 00:33:32.410
they probably literally would have killed,

00:33:32.410 --> 00:33:34.610
to learn the knowledge
that you get to learn

00:33:34.610 --> 00:33:36.330
over the course of your schooling,

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:38.106
and to realize, that, you know,

00:33:38.106 --> 00:33:40.081
don't put it all on other people

00:33:40.081 --> 00:33:41.279
to make it interesting for you.

00:33:41.279 --> 00:33:43.100
Don't put it all on other people

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:45.606
to kinda spoon-feed the knowledge to you.

00:33:45.606 --> 00:33:48.590
This is all about you really owning it,

00:33:48.590 --> 00:33:52.620
and kinda owning the hill,
I'm using a skiing analogy,

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:53.700
because I'm not a great skier,

00:33:53.700 --> 00:33:55.960
but that kind of was an unlocking for me,

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:57.820
where it was just like,
don't be afraid of the hill,

00:33:57.820 --> 00:33:59.760
own the hill, this is your hill.

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:03.270
I'm still a horrible skier,
but have that same mindset

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:04.884
when you're learning something.

00:34:04.884 --> 00:34:06.500
Don't put it on the teachers,

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:08.490
like, "Hey, teacher, make
this interesting for me,

00:34:08.490 --> 00:34:09.600
otherwise I'm gonna check out."

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:10.690
That's not fair to the teacher,

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:13.420
because you say, "Hey,
I'm gonna put it on myself

00:34:13.420 --> 00:34:14.253
to make it interesting."

00:34:14.253 --> 00:34:15.940
And there's more resources than ever.

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:16.773
You have you're textbooks,

00:34:16.773 --> 00:34:18.450
you have your incredible teachers,

00:34:18.450 --> 00:34:20.510
but you also have resources
like Khan Academy.

00:34:20.510 --> 00:34:22.850
You know if you wanna know
why something is useful,

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:24.210
things like, on YouTube now,

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:25.260
there's someone who's made a video

00:34:25.260 --> 00:34:26.550
about pretty much everything

00:34:26.550 --> 00:34:28.620
on an enrichment side of things.

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:30.880
So, you know, the world's your oyster,

00:34:30.880 --> 00:34:33.810
it's really for you to make
it what you wanna make of it.

